// An array fill literal holding a counted reference adds one owner for every
// element it makes, and this asserts the number.
//
// The count is how many owners exist, which region_success establishes for a
// single binding: an lvalue keeps its reference, so copying from one adds an
// owner, while a temporary hands over the reference it was born holding and
// adds none. A fill '[n; value]' stores one value into n elements, so the amount
// is n from an lvalue and n-1 from a temporary, and the non-fill case is exactly
// n = 1. Flow analysis injects that amount once, ahead of the elements, and
// generation calls the region's 'alias' that many times: written out in line
// for a small amount, and as a loop for a large one.
//
// The amount is the whole claim and no run could see it: too low frees the
// allocation while the array still points at it, too high leaks it, and stdout
// shows neither. So the generated IR is the only place the number is visible,
// and the checks in cases.toml read it there: the loop's trip count for the two
// fills of twenty, and for the fill of three the three increments 'alias'
// writes out. That is why this stays a 'compile' scenario carrying checks
// rather than becoming a 'run'.
//
// Neither allocation below is ever released. An array whose elements are owning
// references is not walked at scope exit -- flowScopeDealias visits variables
// whose own type is an owning reference, and an array's is not -- so each leaks.
// Counting n owners is still what makes that a leak rather than a use after
// free, which is what the count is for.

mod region_fill_count;

fn fillFromLval() {
  imm r = +rc-mut 7i32;
  imm arr = [20; r];
}

fn fillFromTemp() {
  imm arr = [20; +rc-mut 8i32];
}

fn fillFewFromLval() {
  imm r = +rc-mut 9i32;
  imm arr = [3; r];
}
